  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 157 AM CDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\nGOES nighttime microphysics RGB shows fog developing across \nLake County IL into extreme southeast WI. Given the fog is \ndeveloping a couple hours earlier than last night and with \nsimilarly favorable conditions to early Tuesday morning, seems \nlikely that dense fog will continue to develop and expand \nthrough sunrise. The northern and northwest suburbs seem most \nlikely for dense fog development, but given the low dewpoint \ndepressions, calm winds, and clear skies, fog will probably \nexpand south into the heart of the metro too, though confidence \nis low in how far south it develops. A dense fog advisory will \nlikely be issued sooner rather than later and will probably \nneed to be expanded as fog expands. Similar to Tuesday morning, \nfog should burn off by around 14z.\n\nVery little day to day change in the weather is expected through\nthe upcoming weekend. A very weak southwest gradient today may\nlimit the inland penetration of the lake breeze this afternoon\ncompared to Tuesday. This would have implications on areal \ncoverage of fog tonight, as the added moisture from the marine\nlayer has been contributing to the fog development. Temperatures\nwill likely be a couple degrees warmer today. \n\nAnother weak surface high will track across the upper Great\nLakes in SE Canada Thursday into Friday, likely resulting in a\nvery weak easterly gradient favoring a deeper inland penetration\nof the weak lake breeze Thursday and possibly Friday as well.\nThis could tee things up for a bit more areal coverage of dense\nfog Fri and Sat morning. \n\nThe omega block and the amplitude of the upper ridge is still\nprogged to break down early next week which at least opens the\ndoor to the possibility of a cold frontal passage and perhaps\nsome rain at some point early next week. Medium range guidance\noften struggles with the breaking down of blocking patterns and\nthere is still considerable ensemble spread, so the magnitude\nand timing of any potential return to more seasonable\ntemperatures is still a low confidence item. As is any potential\nincrease in rain chances. \n\n- Izzi",
